---
import { version } from '../../../package.json';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/scmmishra/clue';
const install = 'curl -fsSL https://clue.shivam.dev/install | bash';
const title = 'clue, a fast and simple coding agent for Claude';
const description = "A fast and simple coding agent for Claude, in your terminal. clue calls Claude's API directly.";

// Medians from benchmarks/README.md.
const waits = [
	{ label: 'Start to a ready prompt', time: '7.5 ms' },
	{ label: 'Resume to your last session', time: '13.8 ms' },
	{ label: "First token, clue's time", time: '5.4 ms' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
			<main>
				<div class="hero">
					<h1>A fast, simple coding agent for Claude</h1>

					<!-- The whole line copies the command. -->
					<button class="install" type="button" data-copy={install}>
						<code data-command><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">$</span> {install}</code>
						<span class="copy">
							<span aria-live="polite">copy</span>
							<kbd aria-hidden="true">[c]</kbd>
						</span>
					</button>
					<div class="meta">
						<p>v{version} (<a href="/changelog">notes</a>) · macOS and Linux · actively developed, so things may change</p>
					</div>
					<p class="note billing">
						clue calls Claude's API through the API SDK, not the Agent SDK, so it bills per token. <code>clue usage</code> shows
						what it has cost.
					</p>

					<section class="waits">
						<h2>Three waits, measured</h2>
						<dl>
							{
								waits.map(({ label, time }) => (
									<div>
										<dt>{label}</dt>
										<dd>{time}</dd>
									</div>
								))
							}
						</dl>
						<p class="note">
							Medians on one Apple silicon Mac. The first-token figure uses a local stand-in for the API, so it
							shows only clue's own time. <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/benchmarks/README.md`}>benchmarks</a>
						</p>
					</section>
				</div>

				<figure class="window">
					<figcaption class="bar">
						<span class="lights" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
						<span>~/code/app</span>
					</figcaption>
					<pre class="terminal" tabindex="0" aria-label="A clue session that fixes a flaky spec"><span class="dim">$</span> clue
<span class="dim">clue {version} · /help for commands</span>

<span class="mark">┃</span> the conversation spec is flaky, fix it

  I'll run the spec a few times to see how it fails.

<span class="mark">●</span> <span class="soft">3 tool calls · 1 command · 1 read · 1 search</span>
<span class="dim">├</span> <span class="soft">Ran bundle exec rspec spec/models/conversation_spec.rb</span>
<span class="dim">├</span> <span class="soft">Read spec/models/conversation_spec.rb  180 lines</span>
<span class="dim">└</span> <span class="soft">Searched "last_activity_at"  12 matches</span>

  The spec compares `last_activity_at` with `Time.now`. The two
  timestamps can land in different seconds. I'll freeze time in that
  example.

<span class="mark">●</span> <span class="soft">2 tool calls · 1 edit · 1 command</span>
<span class="dim">├</span> <span class="soft">Edited spec/models/conversation_spec.rb</span>  <span class="add">+3</span> <span class="remove">−1</span>
<span class="dim">└</span> <span class="soft">Ran bundle exec rspec spec/models/conversation_spec.rb</span>

  Fixed. The example now freezes time, and 5 runs in a row pass.

  <span class="dim">41s · $0.21 · 48k in · 1.2k out</span>

<span class="mark">┃</span>

<span class="dim">auto · opus 5.5 · medium                               ~/code/app (main)</span></pre>
				</figure>

				<div class="features">
					<div class="feature-group">
						<h2 class="group-title">Fast</h2>

						<section>
							<h2>You can type right away</h2>
							<p>clue shows the prompt first. The SDK, MCP servers and skills load in the background.</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>Resuming shows the last screen</h2>
							<p>
								<code>clue -c</code> prints only the last screen of the conversation. While you type, clue sends the
								history to Claude, so its cache is ready when you press enter.
							</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>The same system prompt every time</h2>
							<p>The system prompt and tool list are identical in every session, so Claude reads them from its cache.</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>Long sessions send only what's new</h2>
							<p>Each request carries the messages since Claude's last reply. Claude's API keeps the rest of the conversation for a few minutes.</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>Reads start while Claude is still writing</h2>
							<p>A read or search runs as soon as Claude finishes writing that call, before the rest of the reply arrives.</p>
						</section>
					</div>

					<div class="feature-group">
						<h2 class="group-title">Simple</h2>

						<section>
							<h2>No plan mode</h2>
							<p>Say what you want. If clue heads the wrong way, type while it works to steer it.</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>Output goes to your scrollback</h2>
							<p>
								The conversation prints like any other command's output. Each batch of tool calls shows as a short tree,
								and <code>ctrl+o</code> shows their output, diffs and errors.
							</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>Built for modern terminals</h2>
							<p>
								Redraws don't flicker in terminals with synchronized output, such as Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm, iTerm2 and
								Alacritty. Where the terminal supports it, <code>/copy</code> works over SSH, file paths are clickable, and
								you can jump between your prompts.
							</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2><code>/copy</code> takes a request</h2>
							<p><code>/copy the sql</code> copies only that part of the last reply. A small model picks the part.</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>Three permission modes</h2>
							<p><code>auto</code> runs routine work and has risky actions reviewed first. <code>ask</code> asks before each action. <code>full-access</code> runs every action without checks.</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>A second model for hard steps</h2>
							<p>
								Pick a pair in <code>/model</code>, such as <code>sonnet 5.5 + opus</code>. Sonnet does the work, and asks Opus
								before it plans a change or when it's stuck. Each reply's cost includes both.
							</p>
						</section>

						<section>
							<h2>Your existing instructions</h2>
							<p>clue reads <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files, so a project set up for Claude Code works as it is.</p>
						</section>
					</div>
				</div>
			</main>

		<script>
			const COPIED_MS = 1500;
			const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]')!;
			const label = button.querySelector('.copy span')!;
			const command = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-command]')!;
			let reset = 0;

			async function copy() {
				try {
					await navigator.clipboard.writeText(button.dataset.copy!);
				} catch {
					// The browser refused. Select the command so that the reader can copy it by hand.
					getSelection()?.selectAllChildren(command);
					return;
				}
				label.textContent = 'copied';
				button.dataset.copied = '';
				clearTimeout(reset);
				reset = window.setTimeout(() => {
					label.textContent = 'copy';
					delete button.dataset.copied;
				}, COPIED_MS);
			}

			button.addEventListener('click', copy);
			addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
				if (event.key !== 'c' || event.repeat || event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.altKey) return;
				copy();
			});
		</script>
</Base>
